  Last question, then I'll put the design in front of you.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how someone proves who they are. This matters more than usual here because one of the options shares
  infrastructure with notifications.

  - [1mEmail magic link[0m — you enter an email, get a sign-in link. No passwords stored, so no hashing, no reset flow, no
    breach surface. It requires email-sending infrastructure — which, if notifications end up going out by email, you
    were going to build anyway. One investment, two uses.
  - [1mOAuth (Google / GitHub)[0m — lowest infrastructure of the three; you store no credentials and send no mail. Costs an
    app registration per provider, and ties sign-in to an external service being up. If your team is all on Google
    Workspace or all on GitHub, this is frankly hard to beat.
  - [1mEmail + password[0m — the familiar one, and the one I'd argue against. You inherit password hashing, reset tokens, rate
    limiting, and breach liability, in exchange for nothing the other two don't give you.

  Sequencing note: identity is sub-project one, but nothing stops us building against a [1mdev-only stub[0m (pick a user from
  a dropdown) while the task model comes together, then dropping the real mechanism in behind the same interface. That
  keeps auth from blocking task work without pretending we can skip it. I'd plan for that regardless of which you pick.

  [1mMy recommendation: email magic link[0m, because of the shared-infrastructure argument — if notifications go out by email,
  you build the send path once and it serves both. If you already know notifications will be in-app only and your team
  is on one identity provider, OAuth becomes the better answer.
